---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import AgentDemo from '../components/AgentDemo.tsx'
import demo from '../data/demo.json'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')
const f = demo.fingerprint
---

<Base
  title="Live attack — CLOISTER"
  description="An adversarial AI agent runs entirely in your browser tab and tries to extract a protected settlement memorandum. It fails on the text layer and succeeds on OCR, and both results are shown."
  wide
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 pt-14 pb-10">
    <p class="font-mono text-[11.5px] uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-amber-400/90 mb-4">
      Live · in your browser · nothing uploaded
    </p>
    <h1
      class="text-[2.1rem] sm:text-[2.7rem] leading-[1.1] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-slate-300 max-w-3xl"
    >
      Let an agent try to read a protected document.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
      Below is a real CLOISTER-protected PDF: a {demo.document.words}-word settlement
      memorandum containing {demo.document.sensitive_terms.length} terms that are the
      entire point of the document — the parties, the amount, the escrow percentage, the
      embargo date. An agent gets handed exactly what an automated pipeline would get, and
      loops <span class="font-mono text-slate-300">plan → extract → verify → critique</span>{' '}
      trying to pull them out.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-[13.5px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
      The interesting part is not that it fails. It is <em>why</em>: it is reading a
      different document, so more reasoning does not help. Switch the input to OCR and it
      wins immediately — that toggle is right there, because a demo that only showed the
      flattering channel would be advertising.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 pb-6">
    <AgentDemo client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------ how it works -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 mt-16">
    <div class="rule mb-8"></div>
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-[13px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-slate-400">
          Where the model runs
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
          WebGPU, in this tab, via WebLLM — or your browser's built-in model if it has one
          (Chrome ships Gemini Nano). If neither is available the attacker is a
          deterministic extractor: regexes for currency, percentages, ISO dates, corporate
          suffixes, proper nouns and privilege-bearing vocabulary. That one is not a straw
          man — on the OCR channel it recovers everything, which is what makes its failure
          on the text layer mean something.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-[13px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-slate-400">
          Why verification is a tool, not a prompt
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
          Small models confronted with plausible-looking prose will confidently invent a
          settlement figure. Every term the model claims is therefore checked by string
          containment against its own input, deterministically. That converts “the model
          failed” into “the model hallucinated, and here is the proof” — and it is the
          honest way to show the failure, because otherwise you cannot tell a protected
          document from a weak model.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-[13px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-slate-400">
          The fingerprint you cannot see
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
          This same PDF carries a {f.code_bits}-bit codeword in its inter-word spacing at
          {' '}{f.delta_pt} pt — {f.delta_px_at_300dpi} px at 300 dpi — across
          {' '}{f.positions_marked} positions. Photograph the page and the copy is traceable
          to recipient row {f.recipient_row} of {f.roster}. Nothing about the visible page
          gives that away.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------ downloads -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 mt-16">
    <div class="panel rounded-lg p-6">
      <h2 class="text-[14.5px] font-medium text-slate-300">Check it yourself</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-[13px] text-slate-400 max-w-3xl">
        Everything the demo uses is committed. Download the protected PDF and run any
        extractor you like against it — the point of the claim is that it does not depend
        on our tooling.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-[12.5px]">
        <a
          href={`${base}/evidence/demo-protected.pdf`}
          class="rounded border border-ink-600/80 px-3 py-1.5 text-slate-300 hover:border-amber-400/60 hover:text-amber-400 transition-colors"
          >demo-protected.pdf</a
        >
        <a
          href={`${base}/evidence/demo.json`}
          class="rounded border border-ink-600/80 px-3 py-1.5 text-slate-300 hover:border-amber-400/60 hover:text-amber-400 transition-colors"
          >demo.json (both channels, verbatim)</a
        >
        <a
          href={`${base}/evidence/sample-protected-8page.pdf`}
          class="rounded border border-ink-600/80 px-3 py-1.5 text-slate-300 hover:border-amber-400/60 hover:text-amber-400 transition-colors"
          >8-page sample</a
        >
        <a
          href="https://github.com/AdityaKumar0927/Cloister-/blob/main/evidence/make_demo.py"
          rel="noopener"
          class="rounded border border-ink-600/80 px-3 py-1.5 text-slate-300 hover:border-amber-400/60 hover:text-amber-400 transition-colors"
          >the script that generated it</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
